[405] A conjugate described herein can comprise one or more linkers. In some embodiments, the linker covalently attaches the peptide with the metal chelator. In some embodiments, the peptide attaches directly to the metal chelator without a linker. In some embodiments, the linker covalently attaches the peptide with the covalently bound radionuclide. In some embodiments, the peptide attaches directly to the covalently bound radionuclide without a linker. In some embodiments, the covalently bonded radioisotope is attached to the radiolabeled conjugate through a chemical linker. In some embodiments, a radiopharmaceutical conjugate described herein can comprise one or more linkers connecting one or more covalent radionuclides to the peptide. The one or more linkers can each independently bind a covalent radioisotope. In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is selected from a radioisotope in Table 7 labeled "covalent". In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is selected from fluorine- 18 ( \(^{18}\mathrm{F}\) ), iodine- 131 ( \(^{131}\mathrm{I}\) ), iodine- 123 ( \(^{123}\mathrm{I}\) ), iodine- 124 ( \(^{124}\mathrm{I}\) ), iodine- 125 ( \(^{125}\mathrm{I}\) ), or astatine- 211 ( \(^{211}\mathrm{At}\) ). In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is \(^{131}\mathrm{I}\) . In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is \(^{124}\mathrm{I}\) . In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is \(^{125}\mathrm{I}\) . In some embodiments, the covalent radioisotope is \(^{211}\mathrm{At}\) .  

