## Radionuclide  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 659, 905, 927]]<|/det|>
[472] In one aspect, disclosed herein are radiopharmaceutical conjugates comprising a radionuclide. In some embodiments, the radionuclide is chelated or bound to a metal chelator. In some embodiments, the radionuclide is covalently bound to the conjugate. Generally, the type of radionuclide used in a therapeutic radiopharmaceutical can be tailored to the specific type of cancer, the type of targeting moiety (e.g., binding peptide), etc. Radionuclides that undergo \(\alpha\) - decay produce particles composed of two neutrons and two protons, and radionuclides that undergo \(\beta\) - decay emit energetic electrons from their nuclei. Some radionuclides can also undergo electron capture and emit auger electrons. In some embodiments, the conjugate comprises an alpha particle- emitting radionuclide. Alpha radiation can cause direct, irreparable double- strand dna breaks compared with gamma and beta radiation, which can cause single- stranded breaks via indirect dna damage. The range of these particles in tissue and the half- life of the radionuclide can also be considered in designing the radiopharmaceutical conjugate. Table 7 below illustrates some properties of exemplary radionuclides.

