<|ref|>sub_title<|/ref|><|det|>[[118, 135, 684, 153]]<|/det|>
## Example C6. Protocol-FACS analysis for compound cell internalization  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[115, 158, 905, 449]]<|/det|>
[757] Cells were dissociated with Accutase and stained with Zombie Violet in PBS (1:1000) at RT in the dark for \(15\mathrm{min}\) . Cells were washed 3 times of cold PBS and resuspended at \(2\times 10^{6}\) cells/mL in FACS buffer and \(90~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of cells were seeded into each well of a 96 wells plate. \(10~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of serial diluted compounds were added to \(90~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of cells in each well and incubated on ice for 1 hour. For each compound concentration, 4 groups of samples were prepared as following: \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) - No Quench; \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) - Quench; \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) - No Quench; and \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) - Quench. Cells were washed 3 times with cold PBS \((\alpha 1,500\) RPM for \(3\mathrm{min}\) ). Cells were then incubated with Streptavidin- AF488 (1:1000 dilution in FACS buffer) on ice for \(30\mathrm{min}\) . Cells were washed 3 times with cold PBS \((\alpha 1,500\) RPM for \(3\mathrm{min}\) ), resuspended in \(100~\mu \mathrm{L}\) PBS and incubated at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) or \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 1- 3 hours. Cells were washed 3 times with cold PBS \((\alpha 1,500\) RPM for \(3\mathrm{min}\) ). No quench groups were incubated in \(50~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of cold FACS buffer and quench groups were incubated with \(50~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of \(30\mathrm{ug / ml}\) of anti- Alexa488 in FACS buffer at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\mathrm{min}\) . Cells were washed two times, resuspended in \(100~\mu \mathrm{L}\) of cold PBS and proceeded to signal acquisition on CytoFlex (Beckman Coulter Life Sciences, Indianapolis, IN).  

<|ref|>text<|/ref|><|det|>[[118, 453, 866, 494]]<|/det|>
[758] Internalization rate of biotinylated compound: EphA2- Biotin- 21 and EphA2- Biotin- 88 were measured in PC3 cells at \(10\mathrm{nM}\) and \(100\mathrm{nM}\) at 2 hour time point (see FIG. 25).  

